60,3,1,talk,George,,"I strongly prefer Option B. It removes the need to ask for favors or negotiate daily, which is exactly what destroys the social harmony of a group.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Toshio,,Option B is the most efficient. It eliminates the administrative burden of tracking every single run and the subsequent friction of auditing figures.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Victoria,,Option B is the only one that guarantees me the car on a midweek morning. I can rely on it being there without fighting for a slot.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Misaki,,"I cannot support Option B. It's the ultimate subsidy; someone who doesn't use the car on their day still pays, while a heavy user on another day gets a bargain.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Minato,,"Option A seems the most honest because it tracks everything, but I'm worried it might make us argue about the numbers again.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,George,,"@Misaki, the ""bargain"" is the price we pay for peace. I'd rather we be slightly unfair with money than constantly clipped with each other in the kitchen.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Minato, your worry is correct. Option A is just a digital version of the same system that failed us. It doesn't remove the auditing; it just moves it to a screen.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Misaki, if we use Option B, we can simply add a rule that the car must be returned clean and fueled by the end of the day. That solves my concern.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"@George, peace bought with unfairness is just suppressed resentment. Option A is the only way to ensure that costs are tied directly to individual usage.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Minato,,"I like the idea of Option B because it sounds peaceful, but I want to make sure the person who pays the most doesn't feel sad.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,George,,"@Minato, the sadness comes from the process of asking and denying. A fixed schedule removes the ""asking"" part entirely. It's the most independent way to live.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"Option C is far too chaotic and puts too much burden on one person. Between A and B, B is the only one that solves the time-waste problem.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Victoria,,I agree with @Toshio. Option A still leaves the car's state to chance. Option B gives us a clear handover point every 24 hours.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Misaki,,"If we choose B, we are essentially agreeing to ignore the actual costs. I believe we are adults who can handle a spreadsheet if it means no one is being taken advantage of.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Minato,,"I think I'll go with the one that makes the most people feel relaxed. If most of you like the fixed days, maybe that's the best way.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
